    rdf:value "\"The monument dates back to mid 14th century. It is built on the burial site of Arghun's daughter. Arghun was the pre-Ilkhanid, Mongol ruler of Iran in the mid 13th century. The structure was destroyed during the earthquake of 1931.\" [Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery Archives, Curatorial Research Assistant]" .

kg:note_ref10443_scopecontent_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    crm:P72_has_language lang:und ;
    rdf:value "- FSg curatorial research specialist remark on Antoin Sevruguin photo condition reads, \"Thick glass. Multiple red dots on the imperfections of the sky.\"" .

kg:note_ref10443_scopecontent_2_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    crm:P72_has_language lang:und ;
    rdf:value "- Scratched handwritten number (inked, probably by Antoin Sevruguin) reads, \"1268.\"" .

kg:note_ref10443_scopecontent_3_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    crm:P72_has_language lang:und ;
    rdf:value "- Myron Bement Smith handwritten caption in English reads, \"47.P; Box 47.2: (P) [black-and-white print on hand]. Azerbaijan. Salmas. Tomb of the daughter of Arghun.\" [Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P: Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran]" .

kg:note_ref10444_arrangement_0_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    crm:P72_has_language lang:und ;
    rdf:value "According to Myron B. Smith handwritten document (Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran), Antoin Sevruguin's 696 glass negatives, at the time of their acquisition, were arranged into 61 boxes without any apparent organization. Today they are housed in archival document boxes, essentially duplicating the original arrangement, and stored on shelves. This glass negative was included into \"Box 47.\"" .

