kg:note_ref10560_scopecontent_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdfs:label "Scope and Contents" ;
    crm:P148_has_component kg:note_ref10560_scopecontent_0_p_0 ;
    crm:P2_has_type mbs:ScopeContent ;
    crm:P72_has_language lang:und ;
    rdf:value "Scope and Contents \"Talar-I Takht or Throne room of Gulistan palace housed the famous Peacock throne and was the offical reception room of the palace. Located on the northwest side of the complex, the room was initially designed as a museum but was later known for its primary use as the official reception room of the palace. The construction of the new complex and the throne room started in 1874 and concluded two years later in 1876. It was then decorated with the extensive collection of foreign gifts and local/Iranian artefacts of the palace. The room is currently used as a museum as well. The photo depicts the room with a few attendants of the court scattered about and in the centre of the room, returning the gaze of the camera. The photographer's camera is placed mid-lenghth of the room, looking away from the placement of the throne to the other side of the room. Other images of the same room in the archives usually depict the room in the opposite direction.\" [Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery Archives, Curatorial Research Assistant]" ;
    mbs:noteType mbs:ScopeContent .

kg:note_ref10560_scopecontent_1 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
