    crm:P3_has_note "Arrangement According to Myron B. Smith handwritten document (Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran), Antoin Sevruguin's 696 glass negatives, at the time of their acquisition, were arranged into 61 boxes without any apparent organization. Today they are housed in archival document boxes, essentially duplicating the original arrangement, and stored on shelves. This glass negative was included into \"Box 26.\"",
        "Bibliography Michael Myers Shoemaker, The Heart of the Orient, New York: The Knickerbockers Press, 1904, p:169",
        "Biographical / Historical Antoin Sevruguin is one of the early pioneers of commercial photography in Iran. He arrived in Iran from Tbilisi, Georgia in the mid 1870s to set up shop in Ala al-Dawla street in Tehran. From the early days, Sevruguin's studio was trusted both by the Qajar court and by foreign visitors to Iran. Highly regarded for their artistic ingenuity outside Iran, Sevruguin's photographs of 'ethnic types,' architecture and landscape, and depictions of daily life of Tehran found their way into foreign travelogues, magazines and books. As such, he stands alone in a relatively large group of early Iranian photographers for being recognized and celebrated outside the boundaries of the country. Antoin Sevruguin passed away in 1933, leaving behind only a fraction of his large collection of glass negatives, which is currently in the Archives of the Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery.",
        "Existence and Location of Copies Gelatin silver print numbered FSA A.4 2.12.Sm.76; available in Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.12: Antoin Sevruguin Photographs",
        "General Title and summary note are provided by Shabnam Rahimi-Golkhandan, FSg curatorial research specialist.",
        "Local Numbers FSA A.4 2.12.GN.26.09",
        "Scope and Contents \"Built in mid 14th century on the road to Mashhad, the tomb houses a Mihrab inside. The photograph is the view towards north and from the southern façade of the structure.\" [Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery Archives, Curatorial Research Assistant]",
        "Scope and Contents - FSg curatorial research specialist remark on Antoin Sevruguin photo manipulation reads, \"There is red paper stuck to the area around the structure and black and red paint covers the area between the paper and the structure. Two figures standing in front of the structure are also painted over with black ink.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Faded handwritten number (inked, probably by Antoin Sevruguin) reads, \"267.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Handwritten information on slip of paper (from a 1943-1944 cash book, produced by the Bathni Brothers, Tehran) reads, \"218) Imamzadeh in Nishapur. [Marked with unidentified seal].\" [Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information]",
        "Scope and Contents - Handwritten number (scratched, probably by Antoin Sevruguin) reads, \"711.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Myron Bement Smith handwritten caption in English reads, \"47.P; Box 26.9 (P) [black-and-white print on hand]. Khorasan. Tus. \"Hauriyaniyeh\" Mausoleum (# 218).\" [Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P: Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran]" ;
    crm:P48_has_preferred_identifier kg:id_ark_ref10202 ;
