kg:component_ref10207 a crm:E73_Information_Object,
        mbs:ArchivalComponent,
        mbs:ItemUnit ;
    rdfs:label "Studio Portrait: Hassan Ali Khan Garusi (b.1822-d.1899), Known as Amir Lashgar and Amir Nizam" ;
    dct:identifier "FSA A.4 2.12.GN.26.14" ;
    dct:language "und" ;
    crm:P102_has_title kg:title_ref10207 ;
    crm:P129_is_about kg:concept_aat_300015524,
        kg:work_early_photography_of_iran ;
    crm:P1_is_identified_by kg:title_ref10207 ;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300134674,
        kg:concept_aat_300134674,
        kg:concept_aat_300223022 ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Arrangement According to Myron B. Smith handwritten document (Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran), Antoin Sevruguin's 696 glass negatives, at the time of their acquisition, were arranged into 61 boxes without any apparent organization. Today they are housed in archival document boxes, essentially duplicating the original arrangement, and stored on shelves. This glass negative was included into \"Box 26.\"",
        "Bibliography Mahdi Bamdad, Sharh-i Hal-i Rijal-i Iran dar Qarn-i 12,13,14 Hijri, Tehran: Intisharat-i Zavvar (Zavvar Publishing): 1992, Vol.1, p: 359-367",
        "Biographical / Historical Antoin Sevruguin is one of the early pioneers of commercial photography in Iran. He arrived in Iran from Tbilisi, Georgia in the mid 1870s to set up shop in Ala al-Dawla street in Tehran. From the early days, Sevruguin's studio was trusted both by the Qajar court and by foreign visitors to Iran. Highly regarded for their artistic ingenuity outside Iran, Sevruguin's photographs of 'ethnic types,' architecture and landscape, and depictions of daily life of Tehran found their way into foreign travelogues, magazines and books. As such, he stands alone in a relatively large group of early Iranian photographers for being recognized and celebrated outside the boundaries of the country. Antoin Sevruguin passed away in 1933, leaving behind only a fraction of his large collection of glass negatives, which is currently in the Archives of the Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery.",
        "General Title and summary note are provided by Shabnam Rahimi-Golkhandan, FSg curatorial research specialist.",
        "Local Numbers FSA A.4 2.12.GN.26.14",
        "Scope and Contents \"Hassan Ali Khan Garusi or Amir Nizam was born in 1822 and from 1838 till his death in 1899 occupied numerous official positions in Qajar court. He was one of the most influential politicians of the Qajar court. The photo is most probably taken in the last years of his life.\" [Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery Archives, Curatorial Research Assistant]",
        "Scope and Contents - Handwritten information on slip of paper (from a 1943-1944 cash book, produced by the Bathni Brothers, Tehran) reads, \"49) Iranian minister ??.\" [Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information]",
        "Scope and Contents - Handwritten number (inked, probably by Antoin Sevruguin) reads, \"878.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Myron Bement Smith handwritten caption in English reads, \"47.P; Box 26.14: Iranian minister.\" [Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P: Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran]" ;
