        "General Title and summary note are provided by Shabnam Rahimi-Golkhandan, FSg curatorial research specialist.",
        "Local Numbers FSA A.4 2.12.GN.53.05",
        "Scope and Contents \"Muavin al-Dawla occupied several trade related positions during the last 20 years of Qajar dynasty in Iran. Amongst his official appointments was the head of trade court in 1892 and the head of Iran's council in Tbilisi from 1894-1896 in Nasir al-Din Shah's time. He later became the minister of trade in Muzaffar al-Din Shah's time. In 1903 he became Iran's diplomatic agent and later council in Balkans and lived in Bucharest, Romania till 1906. He then became the minister of Telegraph and post in 1911 and a year later became the minister of finance. three years later he was appointed minister of foreign affairs. The photo is a studio portrait of Muavin al-Dawla in his fifties and in full regalia.\" [Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery Archives, Curatorial Research Assistant]",
        "Scope and Contents - FSg curatorial research specialist remark on Antoin Sevruguin photo manipulation reads, \"The face is carefully touched up. There is a halo of a the varnish around the face.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Handwritten information on slip of paper (from a 1943-1944 cash book, produced by the Bathni Brothers, Tehran) reads, \"240) Iranian minister.\" [Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information]",
        "Scope and Contents - Myron Bement Smith handwritten caption in English reads, \"47.P; Box 53.5: Iranian minister (240).\" [Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P: Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran]" ;
    crm:P48_has_preferred_identifier kg:id_ark_ref10517 ;
