kg:component_ref10549 a crm:E73_Information_Object,
        mbs:ArchivalComponent,
        mbs:ItemUnit ;
    rdfs:label "Firuzkuh (Iran): Qajar Rock Relief Depicting Fath Ali Shah in Royal Hunt" ;
    dct:identifier "FSA A.4 2.12.GN.56.07" ;
    dct:language "und" ;
    crm:P102_has_title kg:title_ref10549 ;
    crm:P129_is_about kg:concept_lcsh_sh85112527,
        kg:concept_qajar_dynasty_iran_1794_1925,
        kg:work_early_photography_of_iran ;
    crm:P1_is_identified_by kg:title_ref10549 ;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300134674,
        kg:concept_aat_300134674 ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Arrangement According to Myron B. Smith handwritten document (Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran), Antoin Sevruguin's 696 glass negatives, at the time of their acquisition, were arranged into 61 boxes without any apparent organization. Today they are housed in archival document boxes, essentially duplicating the original arrangement, and stored on shelves. This glass negative was included into \"Box 56.\"",
        "Bibliography J. P. Luft, The Qajar Rock Reliefs, Iranian Studies, Vol. 34, No. 1/4, p:33-34",
        "Biographical / Historical Antoin Sevruguin is one of the early pioneers of commercial photography in Iran. He arrived in Iran from Tbilisi, Georgia in the mid 1870s to set up shop in Ala al-Dawla street in Tehran. From the early days, Sevruguin's studio was trusted both by the Qajar court and by foreign visitors to Iran. Highly regarded for their artistic ingenuity outside Iran, Sevruguin's photographs of 'ethnic types,' architecture and landscape, and depictions of daily life of Tehran found their way into foreign travelogues, magazines and books. As such, he stands alone in a relatively large group of early Iranian photographers for being recognized and celebrated outside the boundaries of the country. Antoin Sevruguin passed away in 1933, leaving behind only a fraction of his large collection of glass negatives, which is currently in the Archives of the Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery.",
        "Existence and Location of Copies Gelatin silver print numbered FSA A.6 04.24.017; available in Ernst Herzfeld Papers, Series 4: Photographic Files, 1903-1947.",
        "General Title and summary note are provided by Shabnam Rahimi-Golkhandan, FSg curatorial research specialist.",
        "Local Numbers FSA A.4 2.12.GN.56.07",
        "Scope and Contents \"The Shah is surrounded by twenty-one of his sons and courtiers. In the case of the Qajar princes, the names of each is carved beside their head in the relief. The princes are carved in almost identical attires. The date of construction of the relief is estimated to be the twentieth year of Fath Ali Shah's reign in 1817-18. Fath Ali Shah Qajar was the second king of Qajar dynasty in Iran that rulled the country for around 37 years (1797-1834). He was born to Aqa Mohammad Khan Qajar's brother and since Aqa mohammad Khan did not have a child, Fath Ali shah assumed the position of the king upon his death.\" [Freer Gallery of Art and Arthur M. Sackler Gallery Archives, Curatorial Research Assistant]",
        "Scope and Contents - FSg curatorial research specialist remark on Antoin Sevruguin photo condition reads, \"Top and right edge are chopped off.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Faded handwritten number (inked, probably by Antoin Sevruguin) reads, \"1041.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Myron Bement Smith handwritten caption in English reads, \"47.P; Box 56.7: Carving.\" [Myron Bement Smith Collection, Subseries 2.1: Islamic Archives History, Collection Information; Box 60; Folder 44: 47 P: Antoine Sevruguin, glass negatives, Iran]" ;
    crm:P48_has_preferred_identifier kg:id_ark_ref10549 ;
    crm:P67_refers_to kg:agent_Q382319,
        kg:agent_Q98396885,
        kg:collection_islamic_archives,
